467
00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:35.200
It's incredible what you guys have built.

468
00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:43.200
In 2012, AlexNet was computer vision, image net, image recognition, 82% or something like that, accuracy.

469
00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:48.200
Over the next almost not quite ten years, I think it was like seven years,

470
00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:52.200
every single year, the accuracy error reduced in half.

471
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:53.200
Right?

472
00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:56.200
Every year, the error reduced in half, or otherwise known as Moore's Law.

473
00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:57.200
Okay?

474
00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:02.200
So you double the performance, you double the accuracy, and you double its believability

475
00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:03.200
every single year.

476
00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:06.200
Over the course of seven years, it's now superhuman.

477
00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:10.200
Same thing with speech recognition, same things with natural language understanding.

478
00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:17.200
We want to know, we want to believe, not know, we want to believe that the answer that's being predicted to us is accurate.

479
00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:18.200
We want to believe that.

480
00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:23.200
And so the industry is going to chase that believability or that accuracy

481
00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:28.200
and double its accuracy 2x every year.

482
00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:31.200
I believe that's going to be the same thing with natural language understanding.

483
00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:34.200
And of course, the problem space is a lot more complicated.

484
00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:38.200
But I have every certainty that we're going to double its accuracy every single year,

485
00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:40.200
to the point where it is so accurate.

486
00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:43.200
And we've largely tested across many of your examples when you interact with it,

487
00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.200
that you go, you know what, this is really, really good.

488
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:47.200
I believe the answer that it's producing for me.

489
00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:48.200
That condition is very important.

490
00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:49.200
The second thing is this.

491
00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:56.200
Today's language models, today's AI, and everything that we've shown are one shot.

492
00:26:56.200 --> 00:27:00.200
And yet, you and I both know that there are many things that we think about that are not one shot.

493
00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.200
You have to iterate.

494
00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:04.200
And so how do you reason about a plan?

495
00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:07.200
How do you come up with a strategy to solve a problem?

496
00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:08.200
Maybe you need to use tools.

497
00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:10.200
Maybe you have to look up some proprietary data.

498
00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.200
Maybe you have to do some research, in fact.

499
00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:14.200
Maybe you have to ask another agent.

500
00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.200
Maybe you have to ask another AI.

501
00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:17.200
Maybe you have to be human in a loop.

502
00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:18.200
Ask a human.

503
00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:19.200
Trigger an event.

504
00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:20.200
Send an email to somebody or text to somebody.

505
00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:23.200
Get a response before you can move on to the next step of that plan.

506
00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.200
And so a large language model has to iterate and think of a plan.

507
00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:28.200
That's not a one shot thing.

508
00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:31.200
And once it comes up with a plan, as it traverses that graph,

509
00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:35.200
there's a whole bunch of language models that are going to get instantiated and initiated.

510
00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:39.200
And so I think your future models are going to iterate.

511
00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:44.200
And so instead of just a one shot model, it's going to be a planning model

512
00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:47.200
with a whole bunch of other models around it that are particularly good at particular skills.

513
00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:49.200
And so I think we have long ways to go.

514
00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:54.200
If you're not engaging AI actively and aggressively, you're doing it wrong.

515
00:27:54.200 --> 00:27:56.200
You're not going to lose your job to AI.

516
00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.200
You're going to lose your job to somebody who uses AI.

517
00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:01.200
Your company is not going to go out of business because of AI.

518
00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:04.200
Your company is going to go out of business because another company used AI.

519
00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:05.200
There's no question about that.

520
00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.200
And so you have to engage AI as quickly as possible.

521
00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:12.200
You have to engage AI as quickly as possible so that you can do things that you think cost too much to do.

522
00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:17.200
For example, if the marginal cost of intelligence was practically zero,

523
00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.200
there are a lot of things that you would do now that you wouldn't have done otherwise.

524
00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.200
And so notice how often we do search.

525
00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.200
And these days notice how often we ask questions.

526
00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:28.200
And even if I know the answer, I'll just ask it anyways.

527
00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:30.200
You know, just to see what it comes up with.

528
00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:33.200
And so I think we want that to happen.

529
00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:38.200
We want the marginal cost of these type of activities to be as low as possible so that you use it in abundance.

530
00:28:38.200 --> 00:28:46.200
Second, if you could use AI to be productive, you know that productive companies leads to higher earnings.

531
00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:48.200
Higher earnings leads to more employment.

532
00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:50.200
More employment leads to more social growth.

533
00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:53.200
And so there's a lot of reasons to want to drive productivity into companies.

534
00:28:53.200 --> 00:29:00.200
Apart from just changing your manufacturing plans and your CapEx plans, how has AI changed how NVIDIA works internally?

535
00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:05.200
We were one of the first technology companies to invest in our own AI supercomputers.

536
00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:08.200
We can't design a chip anymore without AI.

537
00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:14.200
At night, our AIs are exploring design spaces, vast and wide, that we would never do ourselves.

538
00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:15.200
Because it costs too much money to explore.

539
00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:20.200
And so we, our chips are so much better.

540
00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:23.200
Because of an AI, we can reduce the amount of energy used for our chips.

541
00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:24.200
It's higher performance.

542
00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:28.200
Our software, we can't write software without AI anymore.

543
00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:32.200
We have to explore all the, you know, the design space of optimizing compilers is too large.

